{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 2</p>
  <h1>Data</h1>
  <p class="lede">Templates show what Roc hands them. <code>&#123;&#123; name &#125;&#125;</code> is a
  field of a record your Roc code builds.</p>
</header>

<figure class="preview">
  <figcaption><code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>, rendered</figcaption>
{{> Hello hello}}</figure>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <p>Open <code>main.roc</code>. Near the top, under <em>Your turn</em>:</p>
  <pre><code>you = "friend"</code></pre>
  <p>Put your own name there. Save. This one is Roc, so roc compiles it and the app
  restarts: about a second, said in the terminal.</p>
  <p>Then try <code>you = "&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;"</code>. The page shows it as
  text: a value is HTML-escaped unless the template asks for it raw, with three braces.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The contract</h2>
  <p>roux read <code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>, saw it reads <code>name</code>, and wrote a Roc
  module for it beside the template. This is <code>templates/Hello.roc</code> as it is right now:</p>
  <pre class="file"><code>{{ generated }}</code></pre>
  <p><code>Ctx</code> is the template's <em>contract</em>: the record it reads.
  <code>Hello.template(ctx)</code> makes the template's value, and the page you are reading
  passes <code>hello</code> to it. Nothing is rendered in Roc: the value is data, and the
  server renders it as the response goes out.</p>
  <p><code>main.roc</code> imports it as <code>templates/Hello</code>. roux rewrites it only when the contract changes. Markup edits leave
  it, and your Roc, alone. That is the next lesson.</p>
</section>
